---
import Gamescale from '../components/Gamescale.astro';
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';

const DESCRIPTION =
  'gamescale runs a game at 1× so XWayland hands it the panel’s real mode, then puts the desktop back when it exits — including when it doesn’t exit cleanly.';

const FLATPAK_ERR =
  '$ flatpak run --command=sh --filesystem=/usr/bin/gamescale:ro com.valvesoftware.Steam\nF: Not sharing "/usr/bin/gamescale" with sandbox: Path "/usr" is reserved by Flatpak';
const SETUP = 'pulsar setup gamescale --platform steam';
---

<Base title="gamescale — the panel’s real resolution, for one game at a time" description={DESCRIPTION} path="/gamescale">
  <main id="main" class="page">
    <arc-container size="lg">
      <arc-page-header border heading="gamescale" description="The panel’s real resolution, for one game at a time.">
        <img slot="above" class="gs-icon" src="/assets/gamescale.svg" alt="" width="48" height="48" />
      </arc-page-header>

      <section class="chapter">
        <Gamescale />
      </section>

      <section class="chapter" id="in-the-image">
        <h2>What ships in the image</h2>
        <arc-divider class="h-rule" align="left" variant="line-gradient"></arc-divider>
        <div class="two">
          <p>
            <a href="https://github.com/arclight-digital/gamescale">gamescale</a> ships at a pinned,
            hash-verified tag: the script at <code>/usr/bin/gamescale</code>, its top-bar indicator
            enabled system-wide, and a reconcile unit that restores your scale if a game dies
            without cleaning up.
          </p>
          <p>
            For <strong>native</strong> launchers and terminal use that is everything, and a
            machine that never installs anything still recovers its scale after a crash.
          </p>
        </div>
      </section>

      <section class="chapter" id="flatpak">
        <h2>Flatpak launchers need one more step</h2>
        <arc-divider class="h-rule" align="left" variant="line-gradient"></arc-divider>
        <div class="two">
          <div>
            <p>
              For <strong>Flatpak</strong> launchers it is not enough, and the reason is categorical
              rather than a papercut. Flatpak reserves <code>/usr</code> for the runtime and refuses
              to share the host's:
            </p>
            <arc-code-block class="theme-fixed-dark" language="bash" filename="flatpak" code={FLATPAK_ERR}></arc-code-block>
            <p>
              The <code>/usr</code> a Flatpak sees is the runtime's, so no grant can ever expose{' '}
              <code>/usr/bin/gamescale</code> to Steam. A copy under <code>$HOME</code> is the only
              kind that maps in, which means granting a launcher always means running the installer:
            </p>
            <arc-code-block class="theme-fixed-dark" language="bash" filename="setup" code={SETUP}></arc-code-block>
          </div>
          <div>
            <p>
              That runs the installer copy staged in the image — offline, pinned, matching the image
              exactly. Upstream's <code>curl | sh</code> works too; it just fetches whatever is
              current rather than what the image pinned.
            </p>
            <p>
              <strong>The install shadows the image copy, and that is the intended outcome.</strong>{' '}
              User paths win every collision: your shell finds <code>~/.local/bin</code> before{' '}
              <code>/usr/bin</code>, GNOME prefers a user extension over a system one with the same
              uuid, and systemd prefers a user unit over <code>/usr/lib/systemd/user</code>. One of
              each runs, never both.
            </p>
            <p class="hint">
              Chasing odd behaviour? <code>gamescale --version</code> tells you which copy you are
              actually running.
            </p>
          </div>
        </div>
      </section>
    </arc-container>
  </main>
</Base>

<style>
  .gs-icon {
    display: block;
  }
  .two {
    display: grid;
    grid-template-columns: repeat(2, minmax(0, 1fr));
    gap: var(--space-xl);
    max-width: 72rem;
    align-items: start;
  }
  .two arc-code-block {
    display: block;
    margin: var(--space-sm) 0 var(--space-md);
  }
  @media (max-width: 56rem) {
    .two {
      grid-template-columns: minmax(0, 1fr);
      gap: var(--space-md);
    }
  }
</style>
